    "verdict": "CONFIRMED at the QUADRATURE level (decisive analytic test on a controlled curved surface with known exact co-area): the h->0 kernel/ladder limit (1.746) equals the exact co-area integral (1.748), and the TWO-AXIS-AVERAGED 1/|gradP|-weighted strict scan converges to the SAME exact co-area (1.74-1.76 as du->0). The naive UNWEIGHTED scan converges instead to the arc-length-measure ratio (~2.07), a definite bias of ~0.33 (~19%). KEY CAVEAT discovered: two-axis averaging is ESSENTIAL for the 1/|gradP| weight to recover co-area. At the REE-OPERATOR level on coarse grids (G<=21, du>=0.4) the weighted fixed point (c) does NOT yet match the ladder (gap 0.145 at G=21) because quadrature convergence to co-area is slow (needs G~400+) and fixed-point composition amplifies residual error; the naive scan bias at G=21 is 0.263. Both strict scans have near-zero deficit (~0.01-0.13) on these grids vs the ladder ~0.28, so neither is a substitute for the ladder at usable resolutions; the ladder is the reliable estimator."
